The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'THELOOK_ECOMMERCE': {'THELOOK_ECOMMERCE': ['DISTRIBUTION_CENTERS', 'ORDERS', 'USERS', 'EVENTS', 'INVENTORY_ITEMS', 'PRODUCTS', 'ORDER_ITEMS']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns & their potential usage  
• ORDER_ITEMS."product_id": join key to PRODUCTS table to know the product that was bought.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."id": each order‐item row represents a single purchased unit (implicit quantity 1).  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status": filter to finished purchases only (e.g., "Complete").  
• PRODUCTS."id": primary key, join to ORDER_ITEMS."product_id".  
• PRODUCTS."category": category for every product; roll‑up purchase quantities by this field.  
• EVENTS."session_id": groups all events from the same browsing session; needed to order events chronologically.  
• EVENTS."created_at": micro‑second epoch timestamp; subtract successive events in the same session to obtain dwell time.  
• EVENTS."uri": the URL visited; product pages usually contain "/products/" (inspect first).  
• EVENTS."event_type": can help isolate page‑view events (e.g., "page_view", "view").  
• EVENTS."user_id": link visits back to purchasers if necessary.  

Typical conditions that may be required  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status" = 'Complete' (or <> 'Cancelled')  
• EVENTS."uri" ILIKE '%/products/%'  (find product‑page visits)  
• Use LEAD() OVER (PARTITION BY "session_id" ORDER BY "created_at") to look at the “next” event in the same session.  
• Convert micro‑seconds to minutes by dividing the time difference by 60 000 000.  

Below are 10 exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each returns ≤ 20 rows) that move from simple inspection to the final analytic step. Replace or adjust filters as you refine understanding of the data.

```sql
-- 1) Description: List 20 distinct product categories available.
SELECT DISTINCT "category"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Count order‑items per category (all statuses) to see volume distribution.
SELECT p."category",
       COUNT(oi."id") AS "purchase_qty"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
JOIN THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS     p
      ON oi."product_id" = p."id"
GROUP BY p."category"
ORDER BY "purchase_qty" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: Top category by completed purchases only.
SELECT p."category",
       COUNT(*) AS "completed_qty"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  oi
JOIN THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS     p
      ON oi."product_id" = p."id"
WHERE oi."status" = 'Complete'
GROUP BY p."category"
ORDER BY "completed_qty" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: See the variety of event types recorded on the website.
SELECT DISTINCT "event_type"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Look at 20 sample URIs that look like product‑detail pages.
SELECT "session_id",
       "created_at",
       "uri",
       "event_type"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS
WHERE "uri" ILIKE '%/product%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: For sessions that include a product‑page visit, show the sequence of events (first 20 rows).
SELECT "session_id",
       "sequence_number",
       "created_at",
       "uri",
       "event_type"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS
WHERE "session_id" IN (
      SELECT DISTINCT "session_id"
      FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS
      WHERE "uri" ILIKE '%/product%'
      LIMIT 1     -- take one session for quick look
)
ORDER BY "session_id", "sequence_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Calculate dwell time (minutes) for each product‑page view by taking the next event in the same session.
SELECT  e."session_id",
        e."uri"                     AS "product_page_uri",
        e."created_at"              AS "start_ts",
        LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at") AS "next_ts",
        (LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at")
         - e."created_at")/60000000.0               AS "minutes_on_page"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS e
WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '%/product%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: Average minutes a user spends on any product page (all categories) across 20 random sessions.
SELECT AVG("minutes_on_page") AS "avg_minutes_any_product"
FROM (
      SELECT  e."session_id",
              (LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at")
               - e."created_at") / 60000000.0 AS "minutes_on_page"
      FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS e
      WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '%/product%'
     )
WHERE "minutes_on_page" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Identify the single category with the highest completed‑purchase quantity.
-- (Returned as one row; use this category in the final step.)
SELECT p."category"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS oi
JOIN THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS    p
      ON oi."product_id" = p."id"
WHERE oi."status" = 'Complete'
GROUP BY p."category"
ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: For that top‑selling category, compute the average minutes users spend per product‑page visit.
--   The sub‑query first isolates product SKUs belonging to the chosen category.
--   Then it matches events whose URI contains those SKUs (heuristic match),
--   finally calculates dwell time and averages it.
WITH top_cat AS (
     SELECT p."category"
     FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS oi
     JOIN THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS p
           ON oi."product_id" = p."id"
     WHERE oi."status" = 'Complete'
     GROUP BY p."category"
     ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
     LIMIT 1
),
prod_skus AS (
     SELECT DISTINCT p."sku"
     FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS p
     JOIN top_cat t
          ON p."category" = t."category"
),
product_events AS (
     SELECT  e."session_id",
             e."created_at",
             LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at") AS "next_ts",
             (LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at") 
               - e."created_at")/60000000.0 AS "minutes_on_page"
     FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS e
     JOIN prod_skus s
          ON e."uri" ILIKE '%' || s."sku" || '%'
     WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '%/product%'
)
SELECT AVG("minutes_on_page") AS "avg_minutes_top_category"
FROM product_events
WHERE "minutes_on_page" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries gradually explore the data needed to solve the stated business question, culminating in a computation of the average time‑on‑page for visits to products in the best‑selling category.
